    "body": [
      "## Tie overtime to the approved roster",
      "Overtime evidence should begin with the approved roster and the actual hours worked, not with a message that says the team stayed late. The specialist can compare the two records, identify the difference. Attach the supervisor's approval. The payroll owner then decides whether the difference is payable and how it should be coded.",
      "Record the date, the worker, the scheduled hours, the additional hours, the reason. The approval. If the additional time was not approved in advance, mark it as an exception rather than silently adding it to the payroll file. The exception should still preserve the facts and route the decision to the named owner.",
      "## Distinguish approval from attendance",
      "A supervisor's approval to work extra hours is not the same as proof that the hours were worked. Keep the approval record and the time record separate, then reconcile them. If the time record shows fewer or more hours than approved, the difference needs a note and a decision rather than an assumption about rounding or intent. Record the reconciliation result so the next cutoff starts from a known state rather than an unresolved question.",
      "This separation also protects the worker. An attendance record can be corrected through the proper process. An approval record shows what the business authorized. When the two disagree, the payroll owner can see both without losing the original evidence.",
      "## Use a cutoff checklist",
      "Before each payroll cutoff, check that every overtime case has a source roster entry, an approval, a time record. A disposition. Confirm that the approval was given by someone with authority for that site or cost center. Flag cases where the approver changed, the worker moved between assignments, or the client requested the extra time directly.",
      "The checklist should be short enough to complete under deadline pressure. Put the highest-consequence checks first, such as unapproved hours, missing time records. Cases that cross a pay period. A specialist who can complete the checklist reliably is more useful than one who writes a long narrative after the cutoff.",
      "## Keep client requests out of the payroll decision",
      "A client may ask for extended coverage. That request does not by itself authorize overtime or change the worker's pay. Route the request to the workforce owner who can decide whether to staff it, negotiate a schedule change, or decline. Record the client request and the internal decision separately so the payroll record shows the authorized basis.",
      "This boundary matters when the client is important or the request is urgent. The assistant can acknowledge the request and gather the operational facts without promising that someone will stay late. The owner can then make a decision that is consistent with the contract, local rules. The worker's schedule.",
      "## Sample the cases that create risk",
      "Review a monthly sample that includes approved overtime, unapproved overtime, overtime that crossed a pay period. Cases where the client requested the extra coverage. Compare the payroll input with the roster and the approval. Record whether the evidence was complete before the cutoff or repaired afterward.",
      "End with one process change and one owner. If late approvals are the main problem, move the approval deadline earlier. If missing time records are the issue, improve the source system or the daily check. The goal is not zero overtime; it is overtime that can be explained and verified.",
      "## Record the reason in plain language",
      "A payroll code explains how hours are treated. It does not explain why the work was necessary. Add a short reason that names the client request, the coverage gap, or the operational event that caused the extra time. Keep the reason factual and avoid speculation about whether the work was avoidable.",
      "The reason helps the owner review patterns later. If most overtime comes from the same client request or the same late approval, the fix may be a schedule change or a clearer cutoff. A code alone makes those patterns hard to see. A short reason makes the next review more useful.",
      "## Give the payroll reviewer a clean handoff",
      "At the cutoff, give the payroll reviewer one packet with the approved roster, the time record, the approval note. The exception list. Put the unresolved items first so the reviewer knows what cannot be processed yet. Do not bury a missing approval inside a long narrative that the reviewer has to interpret.",
      "The handoff should also name the person who can answer a question during the payroll window. If the reviewer has to search for the supervisor, the packet is incomplete. A clean handoff reduces the chance that an unapproved hour is paid by default or an approved hour is missed because the evidence was hard to find. A named contact also helps the reviewer resolve a question without restarting the approval process.",
      "## Review the cost of late corrections",
      "Corrections after payroll consume time from the payroll team and can create uncertainty for the worker. Track how often a correction was needed, how long it took. Whether the cause was a late approval, a missing time record, or a roster mismatch. Use the pattern to fix the earliest point in the process.",
      "A correction is not automatically a failure. Some facts only become clear after the cutoff. The question is whether the team could have known earlier. If the same cause appears repeatedly, change the approval deadline or the daily check instead of asking the payroll reviewer to absorb the extra work.",
      "## Close the pay period with a summary",
      "At the end of the pay period, give the owner a one-page summary of approved overtime, unapproved exceptions, corrections. Open questions. Keep the summary tied to the source records rather than repeating every detail. The owner can then see whether the process is improving or whether the same site keeps creating late approvals.",
      "The summary should not become a second payroll system. Its purpose is to make the decisions visible and to point to the evidence. If a number in the summary disagrees with payroll, the source record wins and the summary is corrected before it is used. The corrected summary should keep a short note explaining what changed and why."
    ]
